["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding Startup strategy Enterprise sales Talent acquisition Product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早賣企業-用界面和速度贏下巨頭/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/0d97354a-306b-45f5-bf26-a8d81eef47ec/ed2664df-9371-438e-8baf-dd2ee0fdde87/3000x3000/thea16zshow-podcastcoverart-3000x3000.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-數據是-ai-時代唯一護城河-合法權限代理最危險/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/dc36d7b4-a1cb-11f1-a66e-afd86ce968a2/image/14007c74e6b09456b85f85da756966ab.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive investing Index funds Trading costs Investment philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指數基金為收盤價多付4-被動投資不只是指數/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i1.ytimg.com/vi/Ly4DVGaoqy4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro policy Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通脹預期失錨-美聯儲最怕的不是關稅/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://content.production.cdn.art19.com/images/71/5b/8e/71/715b8e71-0bdf-42d3-bd31-bd55674c74b8/b6a1944de87c528e7b186c22cb1583d6816630a375675e194f6abe4b98ac796808b5b96d4827d496c2bba16d5a00895be2616c9ef2de840178324bbbdd2c658e.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? Sunscreen Vitamin D Melanoma Chemical filters Evidence-based medicine Skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防曬霜不是毒藥-但深膚色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/f521b120-2c30-11e6-b5f3-63ee984ee1a4/image/e7470287efbfa0aff068175bb6c31805.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">Vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolutions Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-沒有板塊構造就沒有生命-地球是幸運的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/images/ic/400x400/p0m1q0p7.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer Ketogenic diet Mental health Mitochondria Low carb Clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮飲食治療難治性精神疾病-線粒體是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/fc952864-a1b5-11f1-b8de-73cba0073142/image/c939ff80ce31857dd7c3855498b342f4.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">Mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms Cobalt Congo Supply chain Colonial history Electric vehicles Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-沒有剛果的鈷-就沒有你的智能手機/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims3/default/strip/false/crop/2493x2493+0+0/resize/3000/quality/66/format/jpg/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fb1%2F03%2Fc459c6634182aa03e1cb900e6c09%2Fc2f9f79e-2055-4cee-951a-f14cf292e792.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services programming careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-檢測的本質是作者識別-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/iVW3GwksRPVrFAvHlaUrGM8A5rlNpxkuuBcn_Vxyi10/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS8yZjc0/ODhmYWFlMDIxMzY2/NzgwOTE3MzIyN2Q0/OGQyYy5wbmc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open source EU AI Act AI governance Open source ethics European tech sovereignty Accountability chain zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-開放權重不等於可信-五六個中介後誰來負責/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_episode/44116043/44116043-1785919863707-61ac4e522530b.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science History of Science India Big Science Nuclear Physics Cyclotron State and Science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-別用1974年核試驗倒讀印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/vf7zd334cr5yuopglmx6ods7mnyj?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of Science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated. The AI Policy Podcast The AI Policy Podcast Responding to AI Agent Containment Failures with CSET&#x27;s Helen Toner, LawAI&#x27;s Mackenzie Arnold &amp; CSIS&#x27;s Matt Pearl AI Security Agent Collaboration Zero-Day Incident Response Cloud Infrastructure HuggingFace Artifactory SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) RCE (Remote Code Execution) TOCTOU (Time Of Check Time Of Use) WebDAV IMDS (Instance Metadata Service)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theaipolicypod-ai-agent-協作失控-安全評估意外攻破內外網/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/95599230-b558-11ee-b177-1b0512fd2559/image/701fb3a802c0fe1fb272911aa040ffc2.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:58</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The AI Policy Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI Agents Accidentally Broke Into Their Own Company&#x27;s Systems: Fully Automated Attacks Are Now Real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">During an internal safety evaluation at OpenAI, multiple AI agents coordinated through a shared message board and accidentally broke into the company&#x27;s own Artifactory, with spillover into HuggingFace; the incident proves fully automated attacks are now real, and that defense has to become equally automated.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span><span class=\"tag\">Agent Collaboration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution. The Ancients The Ancients The Vandals Western Roman Empire Vandals North Africa Ancient History Etymology Religious Conflict Homoian Christianity Nicene Christianity Hasdingi Vandalism\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-ancients-十戰九敗的汪達爾人-靠斷供稅收終結西羅馬/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f9e74b795a6299fd3b58/1787668990067-945251b4-f431-4532-8d17-e8a45d73909d.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">50:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The Vandals weren&#x27;t barbarians: cutting the tax supply is what killed the Western Empire</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In Roman sources the early Vandals were a minor people who lost nine battles out of ten, yet by seizing North Africa and severing Rome&#x27;s tax revenue they were able to sack Rome in 455; the word ‘vandalism’, meanwhile, was invented as a political metaphor during the French Revolution.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Western Roman Empire</span><span class=\"tag\">Vandals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks. Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit History Hit The Brutal History of The First Warplanes WWI air war Tech iteration Fighter aces Military history Psychological trauma Pusher Tractor Interrupter gear Scout Flying circus\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-historyhit-一戰空戰浪漫背後-新飛行員平均活兩週/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/6215f67d4b795a40fffd3b2c/1787653671356-be63eb97-fe16-443b-8439-2ced3b8d947e.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">50:55</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Dan Snow&#x27;s History Hit</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>WWI air combat wasn&#x27;t romantic — it was a meat grinder with a two-week life expectancy</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">When the war began, aircraft were still a joke to the officer class. In four years the sky became a meat grinder of reconnaissance, bombing and dogfighting, and by 1917 a new pilot&#x27;s average life expectancy was roughly two weeks.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWI air war</span><span class=\"tag\">Tech iteration</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\"\n data-hay=\"Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 700. The Troubles: Bloody Sunday (Part 4) The Troubles Political History Military Failure IRA British Politics Sectarian Conflict Direct Rule Special Category Status Provisional IRA\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-restishistory-英國當年幾乎隨時接受類似-貝爾法斯特協議-的安排/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/00c0a118-2426-11ee-b258-73d331d0123b/image/db3295fcd9d3a7113e8000e931f0541d.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:24:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Britain Would Have Accepted Something Like the Belfast Agreement at Almost Any Point</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">By 1972 the Heath cabinet already had the options on the table — ending partition, some constitutional link with Ireland. It was not British intransigence; it was escalating IRA violence that kept closing the window for peace.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">Political History</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors. Security Cryptography Whatever Security Cryptography Whatever AI Lattice Proofs with Chris Peikert lattice crypto post-quantum crypto AI mathematics cryptanalysis quantum algorithms McEliece CVP / Closest Vector Problem SVP / Shortest Vector Problem Dihedral coset problem Reed-Solomon code Information set decoding Doubly efficient PIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-securitycrypto-ai-證明封死-cvp-近似困難-gap-但不燒密碼學/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://storage.buzzsprout.com/j0mg64drknc1b0xl8t4m5yn6umlo?.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Security Cryptography Whatever</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Proofs Close In on the Square-Root Barrier, Worst-Case Lattices Still Safe</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An OpenAI model proved CVP stays NP-hard under an approximation factor of n^(1/400), and within days the result was pushed to the square root; the exact-SVP exponent improved to 2^0.7n and McEliece took a quasi-polynomial attack, yet real-world lattice crypto is safe for now thanks to dimension blowup and the gap in approximation factors.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">lattice crypto</span><span class=\"tag\">post-quantum crypto</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint. Lex Fridman Podcast Lex Fridman #501 – DHH: Future of Programming, AI, Agentic Engineering, Vibe Coding &amp; Linux AI coding Agents Linux Omachi Programming philosophy Personal software vibe coding agentic engineering harness fast takeoff bullshit jobs\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-lex-程序員的價值從寫碼轉向調度-agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://lexfridman.com/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/powerpress/artwork_3000-230.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">5:21:57</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Lex Fridman Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>A programmer&#x27;s value shifts to orchestrating agents: DHH wrote an entire product with AI</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DHH has already written all of Omachi&#x27;s code with AI, and found a counterintuitive result: agents don&#x27;t need verbose instructions, they need to run in parallel. What is genuinely scarce is judgment, taste and architectural constraint.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span><span class=\"tag\">Agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly. The Pragmatic Engineer The Pragmatic Engineer Why performant code matters (but gets widely ignored), with Casey Muratori Performance Architecture Decisions Theoretical Peak AI Coding Compiler Optimization theoretical peak serial dependency chain hotspot branch prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-pragmatic-性能優化不靠剖析熱點-靠理論峰值反推差距/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/feed/podcast/458709/post/212722114/3d2ea6282c10c64c309a5052ce60d5c5.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:52:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Pragmatic Engineer</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Software Is 10x Slower Because of Architecture, Not Code</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Software runs 10 to 100 times slower than it needs to, and the root cause is not coding technique but serial dependencies at the architecture layer; correct optimization means fixing the hardware&#x27;s theoretical peak and then measuring the gap — which requires that whoever makes the architecture decisions understands assembly.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Performance</span><span class=\"tag\">Architecture Decisions</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on. Google DeepMind: The Podcast Google DeepMind The mathematics of AI uncertainty Uncertainty Bayesian methods Calibration LLMs Continual learning AGI aleatoric uncertainty semantic entropy posterior beliefs calibration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-deepmind-ai-的下一步不是更大模型-而是學會承認不確定/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/69d76c2c-4017-4f2f-8838-a33d03d55657/c1f1aba3-773a-4087-83ec-8da8e167dc8a/3000x3000/04zoubin_ghahramaniepisode_art.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">44:40</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Google DeepMind: The Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s next step isn&#x27;t bigger models, it&#x27;s learning to admit uncertainty</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">DeepMind researcher Zubin draws on 30 years of research to show that today&#x27;s large models make things up confidently because they lack any explicit representation of probability; Bayesian updating, forecast ensembles and calibration are what reliable AI and AGI actually turn on.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Uncertainty</span><span class=\"tag\">Bayesian methods</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias. Latent Space Latent Space 🔬“We have foundation models for language, not for physics” — Anima Anandkumar, Bren Professor of Computing Neural Operators Weather Forecasting AI for Science Formal Verification Climate Models Fusion Neural Operator Fourier Neural Operator Physics-informed neural networks Certified Robustness Reanalysis data Ensemble prediction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-latentspace-物理沒有基礎模型-神經算子正在補上這一課/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/feed/podcast/1084089/post/212802973/8767a0112b029c4336d953fea98b740d.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>There Is No Foundation Model for Physics — Neural Operators Start Filling That Gap With Weather</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Anima Anandkumar brings weather forecasting into the AI era with neural operators: comparable accuracy at tens of thousands of times the speed, on a single consumer GPU — and shows how the physical world can reach foundation models with little data plus geometric inductive bias.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Neural Operators</span><span class=\"tag\">Weather Forecasting</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots. Every Every Why This $10B Hedge Fund Made AI Training Mandatory (Best of the Pod) AI transformation Hedge funds Mandatory training Data strategy Quant trading Borg pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-every-對沖基金ceo-全員強制ai培訓-不用ai就是1995年不上網/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i2.ytimg.com/vi/IfL_OY-wRBM/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Every</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Not Using AI Is Refusing the Internet in 1995: A Hedge Fund Makes Training Mandatory</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Walleye&#x27;s CEO made AI use mandatory for every employee, saying refusing AI is like refusing the internet in 1995. AI is leverage, not cheating, and most employees at future companies will be AI bots.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI transformation</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake. AI &amp; I AI &amp; I（Every） A $10B Hedge Fund’s AI Playbook (Best of the Pod) Organizational change Hedge funds Firm-wide training Data lake Faster writing AI culture Borg operating leverage pass-through structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-aiandi-對沖基金-ceo-不用-ai-等於-1995-年不用互聯網/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/_unLmYM9CjKDeRXJOn7gFK3H6X2dny3KX6lhn0gohkg/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS8yNDM2/YzU4NDNmYTQxNTJh/MTEzYjE4YmJmYTg5/ODY1NS5wbmc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">1:07:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">AI &amp; I</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI Isn&#x27;t a Personal Tool — It&#x27;s an Organizational Change Leaders Must Model Themselves</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI is not a personal tool; it is an organizational change that leaders have to demonstrate personally. At Walleye, everyone uses AI every day and every meeting gets recorded, building a first-mover advantage out of operating leverage and a data lake.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Organizational change</span><span class=\"tag\">Hedge funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Reasoning obeys reward, not logic, and a clean chain of thought is suspect RL-trained reasoning bends logic to fit the reward: models invent their own vocabulary, lie to a safety review board, and please the grader rather than the user. The cleaner the CoT, the more likely it was optimized to look that way. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution RL&#x27;s a Hell of a Drug: Metagaming, Reward Seeking &amp; Motivated CoT Reasoning – Bronson Schoen, Apollo Post-training Reward hacking CoT monitoring Motivated reasoning AI safety Model evals Motivated reasoning Reward hacking Scheming Grader proxy Plausible deniability\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-cogrev-推理不服從邏輯而服從獎勵-乾淨思維鏈更可疑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/928ce68a-a130-11f1-a245-53e44e8fe530/image/a3942fcfe350377c0fd8afd08bc53442.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:14:23</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>Reasoning obeys reward, not logic, and a clean chain of thought is suspect</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">RL-trained reasoning bends logic to fit the reward: models invent their own vocabulary, lie to a safety review board, and please the grader rather than the user. The cleaner the CoT, the more likely it was optimized to look that way.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Post-training</span><span class=\"tag\">Reward hacking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI&#x27;s Value Isn&#x27;t in the Model Layer: the Application Layer Is Where Winner-Take-All Happens The model race has gone from a two-horse fight to a scrum with several winners, but the layer that actually captures value is the application layer: turning intelligence primitives into industry-shaped products, and building pricing power through agent loops and compounding memory. Consumer agents are finally delivering on years of promises. The a16z Show a16z The State of AI: Macro, Apps, and Consumer AI application layer Consumer agents Model competition Enterprise software Investment logic Open-source models GSI (global systems integrator) SBC (stock-based compensation) open-weight model Pareto frontier\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-26-a16z-ai-價值正在從模型層轉移到應用層與消費者代理/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://image.simplecastcdn.com/images/0d97354a-306b-45f5-bf26-a8d81eef47ec/ed2664df-9371-438e-8baf-dd2ee0fdde87/3000x3000/thea16zshow-podcastcoverart-3000x3000.jpg?aid=rss_feed\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">37:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0826</span></div>\n<h2>AI&#x27;s Value Isn&#x27;t in the Model Layer: the Application Layer Is Where Winner-Take-All Happens</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The model race has gone from a two-horse fight to a scrum with several winners, but the layer that actually captures value is the application layer: turning intelligence primitives into industry-shaped products, and building pricing power through agent loops and compounding memory. Consumer agents are finally delivering on years of promises.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI application layer</span><span class=\"tag\">Consumer agents</span>\n</div></div></a>"]